#====================================================================
# E242 — Compiler-specific Fortran module ABI in Conan packages
#====================================================================

id:                     E242
trigger_tool:           conan|cmake|fortran
phase:                  packaging|consumer-configure|compile
error_signature:        Cannot open module file|module file was created by a different version|Unexpected EOF in module file|requires LLVMFlang

severity:               2
category:               fortran-module-abi-contract
match_keywords:         Fortran, .mod, module file, compiler ID, compiler version, LLVMFlang, tool_requires, package_id_mode, CMake component
title:                  Bind compiler-specific Fortran module files to Conan package identity and consumer CMake gates

root_cause:             Fortran .mod files are compiler-specific binary interfaces, but a normal Conan library package ID and an imported CMake target do not automatically encode the compiler executable that produced them. A consumer can therefore resolve the archive while using an incompatible compiler ID or version, or use the Fortran target without explicitly accepting its compiler contract.
affected_scope:         Conan packages that install precompiled Fortran .mod files, especially mixed C and Fortran libraries whose C component should remain usable without the Fortran toolchain.
verification_cmd:       Verify Conan graph identity uses the exact Fortran tool package in full_mode, then configure positive and negative CMake consumers for matching compiler, missing component, disabled Fortran language, wrong compiler ID, and wrong compiler version.

rec_priority:           1
rec_approach:           compiler-identity-package-and-component-gate
rec_description:        |
  Declare the exact Fortran compiler package as a tool requirement with
  package_id_mode=full_mode in both the producer and Fortran consumer. Export
  the Fortran archive and module files as a separate Conan component. Attach a
  CMake build module that requires an explicit Fortran component, verifies that
  the Fortran language is enabled, and checks the compiler ID and exact version
  before allowing the imported Fortran target to link. Keep the C-only
  component independent of this gate.
rec_command:            |
  self.tool_requires("<fortran-tool>/<version>", package_id_mode="full_mode")
  # Package .mod files with the Fortran archive in a separate component.
  # Export a cmake_build_modules guard that checks COMPONENTS Fortran,
  # CMAKE_Fortran_COMPILER_LOADED, CMAKE_Fortran_COMPILER_ID, and
  # CMAKE_Fortran_COMPILER_VERSION before enabling the Fortran target.
rec_auto_fix:           false
rec_impl:               conan-recipe-and-cmake-build-module

forbid_1_pattern:       package.*\.mod.*without.*(compiler|tool_requires|full_mode)
forbid_1_reason:        Shipping module files without compiler identity permits ABI-incompatible consumers to resolve the package.
forbid_1_consequence:   Downstream compilation can fail with unreadable module diagnostics or, for insufficiently checked formats, consume an incompatible interface.

forbid_2_pattern:       find_package\([^)]*\).*Libxc::xcf03|target_link_libraries\([^)]*xcf03
forbid_2_reason:        A Fortran target must not be usable without an explicit component contract and compiler checks.
forbid_2_consequence:   CMake target discovery appears successful even though the active Fortran compiler cannot consume the installed modules.

